@node sb-process
@section sb-process
@cindex Processes, lightweight
@cindex Actors
@cindex Supervisor

The @code{sb-process} module provides lightweight processes in the
manner of Erlang/OTP on top of @code{sb-fiber}: a process is a fiber
with a mailbox, scheduled cooperatively on a pool of carrier threads.
Processes communicate by message passing only.  They can be linked,
so that one's exit is signaled to the other, and monitored, so that
one's exit is reported to the other as a message.  A registry maps
names to processes.  On top of the primitives, a generic server
behaviour handles calls and casts through methods, and supervisors
start, watch and restart children according to a strategy.

On builds with @code{:sb-process-heaps}, every process owns a heap of
its own by default: messages are copied between heaps, a process
shares no mutable state with any other, and its memory is returned in
one step when it exits.  Without that feature, or for processes spawned
with @code{:heap nil}, messages are passed by reference and processes
share the global heap.

@subsection Scheduler

The scheduler is started on demand by the first @code{spawn}, with one
carrier thread per online CPU.  Each carrier runs processes from a run
queue of its own and steals from the others when idle; a process runs
until it waits for a message, sleeps, yields, or has performed
@code{*reductions-per-slice*} message operations.  Scheduling is
cooperative: a process that neither sends, receives nor yields keeps
its carrier until it does.

@deffn Function start-scheduler &key carriers
Start the scheduler with @var{carriers} carrier threads.  Only needed
to choose the carrier count.
@end deffn

@deffn Function stop-scheduler &key (timeout 5)
Send every process a @code{:kill} signal, wait up to @var{timeout}
seconds for them to exit, and stop the carriers.
@end deffn

@deffn Function scheduler-running-p
@deffnx Function scheduler-carrier-count
Whether the scheduler is running, and with how many carriers.
@end deffn

@deffn Variable *default-carrier-count*
@deffnx Variable *reductions-per-slice*
@deffnx Variable *default-process-stack-size*
@deffnx Variable *default-process-heap*
@deffnx Variable *report-process-errors*
The carrier count when @code{start-scheduler} is not told (@code{nil}
means one per CPU); the number of message operations a process may
perform before it yields; the control stack size of a process fiber;
what @code{spawn} does about a heap when not told (@code{t} or
@code{nil}); and whether a process that exits through an unhandled
error reports it on @code{*error-output*} (@code{nil}, @code{t}, or
@code{:backtrace}).
@end deffn

@subsection Processes

@deffn Function spawn function &key arguments name link monitor heap stack-size binding-stack-size trap-exits
Start a process that applies @var{function} to @var{arguments} and
return it, and the monitor reference when @var{monitor} is true.
@var{function} is a global function or the name of one: a closure
allocated in a process heap cannot be shared and is refused.
@var{arguments} are copied into the new process.  @var{name}
registers the process.  @var{link} and @var{monitor} link the calling
process to the new one or make it monitor it.  @var{heap} is
@code{t} for a heap of the process's own, @code{nil} to share the
global heap, or a list of keyword arguments for
@code{sb-fiber:make-heap}.  @var{trap-exits} makes exit signals arrive
as messages.

When @var{function} returns, the process exits with reason
@code{:normal} and its return values as the exit value.  When it
signals an unhandled error, the process exits with reason
@code{(:error @var{condition})}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function spawn-link function &rest keys
@deffnx Function spawn-monitor function &rest keys
@code{spawn} with @code{:link t}, or with @code{:monitor t}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function self
@deffnx Function current-process
The calling process; @code{current-process} returns @code{nil} on a
thread that is not running one, @code{self} signals
@code{no-current-process-error}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function process-alive-p process
@deffnx Function process-state process
@deffnx Function process-exit-reason process
@deffnx Function process-exit-value process
@deffnx Function process-id process
@deffnx Function process-name process
@deffnx Function process-heap process
@deffnx Function process-info process
@deffnx Function list-processes
Accessors.  The state is @code{:new}, @code{:runnable},
@code{:running}, @code{:waiting} or @code{:exited}.  The exit value is
the list of the function's return values, copied out of the process's
heap.  @code{process-info} returns a plist of the above and more.
@end deffn

@deffn Function exit-process &optional (reason :normal)
Exit the calling process with @var{reason}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function signal-exit target reason
Send an exit signal with @var{reason} to @var{target}, a process or a
registered name, on behalf of the calling process (if any).  A process
that traps exits receives it as the message @code{(:exit @var{from}
@var{reason})}; otherwise it exits with @var{reason} the next time it
runs, except that a @code{:normal} signal from another process is
ignored.  @code{:kill} cannot be trapped and makes the target exit with
reason @code{:killed}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function trap-exits
@deffnx Function (setf trap-exits) enable
Whether the calling process receives exit signals as messages.
@end deffn

@deffn Function process-yield
@deffnx Function process-sleep seconds
Let other processes run; suspend the calling process for
@var{seconds}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function join-process process &key timeout
Wait, on a thread that is not a process, for @var{process} to exit.
Returns its exit reason, its exit value and @code{t}, or three
@code{nil}s after @var{timeout} seconds.
@end deffn

@deffn Function await-process process &key timeout
Wait in the calling process for @var{process} to exit, by monitoring
it.  Returns the exit reason and @code{t}, or @code{nil}, @code{nil}
after @var{timeout} seconds.
@end deffn

@subsection Messages

@deffn Function send target message
Send @var{message} to @var{target}, a process or a registered name,
and return it.  The message is copied into the target's heap.  Sending
to a process that has exited does nothing; sending to a name that is
not registered signals @code{unknown-name-error}.  May be called from
any thread.
@end deffn

@deffn Macro receive (var &key timeout) &body clauses
Selective receive.  Each clause is @code{(@var{test} . @var{body})}:
the oldest message for which some @var{test}, evaluated with @var{var}
bound to the message, is true is removed from the mailbox, and the
body of the first clause whose test it satisfies runs with @var{var}
bound to it.  Messages that match no test stay in the mailbox, in
order.  A clause @code{(:timeout . @var{body})} runs instead if no
matching message arrives within @var{timeout} seconds; without such a
clause a timed-out @code{receive} returns @code{nil}.

@lisp
(receive (m :timeout 5)
  ((and (consp m) (eq (car m) :reply)) (cadr m))
  ((eq m :stop) (exit-process :normal))
  (:timeout (error "no reply")))
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn Function receive-message &key timeout
Return the oldest message and @code{t}, waiting for one; with
@var{timeout}, return @code{nil}, @code{nil} after that many seconds.
A timeout of 0 does not wait.
@end deffn

@deffn Function mailbox-count &optional process
@deffnx Function flush-mailbox
The number of messages waiting for @var{process}; discard every
message waiting for the calling process.
@end deffn

@deffn Function send-after seconds target message
@deffnx Function cancel-timer timer
Send @var{message} to @var{target} after @var{seconds}; returns a
timer.  @code{cancel-timer} returns @code{t} if the timer had not
fired yet.
@end deffn

@subsection Links and monitors

@deffn Function link target
@deffnx Function unlink target
Link the calling process and @var{target}, so that when either exits
the other receives its exit signal (see @code{signal-exit}); undo
that.  Linking to a process that has exited delivers an exit signal
with reason @code{:noproc}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function monitor target
@deffnx Function demonitor ref &key flush
Watch @var{target}: when it exits, the calling process receives the
message @code{(:down @var{ref} @var{process} @var{reason})}.
@code{monitor} returns @var{ref}.  Monitoring a process that has
exited delivers the message at once with reason @code{:noproc}.
@code{demonitor} stops watching; with @var{flush}, it also removes a
pending @code{:down} message for @var{ref} from the mailbox.
@end deffn

@deffn Function make-ref
A fresh reference: a fixnum unique for the life of the image.
@end deffn

@subsection Registry

@deffn Function register name process
@deffnx Function unregister name
@deffnx Function whereis name
@deffnx Function registered
Register @var{process} under @var{name}, a symbol, or remove the
registration; the live process registered as @var{name}, or
@code{nil}; the registered names.  A name is released when its process
exits.  Registering a name held by a live process signals
@code{name-taken-error}.
@end deffn

@subsection Generic server

A generic server is a process whose behaviour is defined by methods
specialized on a @dfn{module} designator, typically a symbol, as OTP's
@code{gen_server} is by a callback module.

@deffn Generic Function init module arguments
@deffnx Generic Function handle-call module request from state
@deffnx Generic Function handle-cast module message state
@deffnx Generic Function handle-info module message state
@deffnx Generic Function terminate module reason state
@code{init} returns @code{(values :ok @var{state})} or @code{(values
:stop @var{reason})}.  @code{handle-call} returns @code{(values :reply
@var{reply} @var{state})}, @code{(values :noreply @var{state})} when
the reply will be sent later with @code{reply}, or @code{(values :stop
@var{reason} @var{reply} @var{state})}.  @code{handle-cast} and
@code{handle-info} (which receives every other message, such as
@code{:down} and @code{:exit} messages) return @code{(values :noreply
@var{state})} or @code{(values :stop @var{reason} @var{state})}.
@code{terminate} is called before the server exits.  The default
methods ignore other messages and do nothing on termination.
@end deffn

@deffn Function start-server module &key arguments name link monitor heap stack-size timeout
Spawn a server process for @var{module} and return it once
@code{init} has run with @var{arguments}.  Signals
@code{server-start-error} if @code{init} returns @code{:stop} or
fails.  The other keyword arguments are those of @code{spawn}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function run-server module &key arguments
Run a server for @var{module} in the calling process.
@end deffn

@deffn Function call server request &key (timeout 5)
@deffnx Function cast server message
@deffnx Function reply from value
@deffnx Function stop-server server &key reason timeout
Send @var{request} to @var{server} (a process or a name) and return
its reply, signaling @code{call-timeout} after @var{timeout} seconds
and @code{call-failed} if the server exits meanwhile; send
@var{message} for @code{handle-cast}; reply to a deferred call;
ask the server to exit after calling @code{terminate}.  @code{call}
and @code{stop-server} may be used from any thread.
@end deffn

@lisp
(defmethod init ((module (eql 'counter)) arguments)
  (values :ok (or (first arguments) 0)))
(defmethod handle-call ((module (eql 'counter)) request from state)
  (declare (ignore from))
  (ecase request
    (:get (values :reply state state))
    (:inc (values :reply (1+ state) (1+ state)))))
(defmethod handle-cast ((module (eql 'counter)) increment state)
  (values :noreply (+ state increment)))

(let ((counter (start-server 'counter :arguments '(10) :name 'counter)))
  (cast 'counter 5)
  (call counter :inc))  @result{} 16
@end lisp

@subsection Supervisors

A supervisor is a server (module @code{supervisor}) that starts its
children from specifications, links to them, traps exits, and restarts
them when they exit.

@deffn Function make-child-spec id function &key arguments restart shutdown name heap stack-size trap-exits
Describe a child.  @var{id} identifies it within its supervisor;
@var{function}, @var{arguments}, @var{name}, @var{heap},
@var{stack-size} and @var{trap-exits} are as for @code{spawn}.
@var{restart} is @code{:permanent} (restart always, the default),
@code{:transient} (only after an abnormal exit) or @code{:temporary}
(never; the child is dropped).  @var{shutdown} is how many seconds to
wait for the child to exit after a @code{:shutdown} signal before
killing it (default 5), or @code{:brutal-kill}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function start-supervisor children &key strategy intensity period name link heap
Start a supervisor for @var{children}, a list of child specs, and
return it once they are running.  @var{strategy} is @code{:one-for-one}
(restart the child that exited), @code{:one-for-all} (stop the others
and restart all) or @code{:rest-for-one} (stop and restart those
started after it).  More than @var{intensity} restarts (default 3)
within @var{period} seconds (default 5) make the supervisor stop its
children and exit with reason @code{:shutdown}.
@end deffn

@deffn Function run-supervisor children &key strategy intensity period
Run a supervisor in the calling process.  This is the function to give
a child spec whose child is itself a supervisor.
@end deffn

@deffn Function stop-supervisor supervisor &key reason timeout
@deffnx Function which-children supervisor
@deffnx Function count-children supervisor
@deffnx Function start-child supervisor spec
@deffnx Function terminate-child supervisor id
@deffnx Function restart-child supervisor id
@deffnx Function delete-child supervisor id
Stop a supervisor and its children; list its children as plists
(@code{:id}, @code{:process}, @code{:restarts}, @code{:spec}); count
them; add and start a child; stop a child, keeping its spec; start a
stopped child; remove a stopped child's spec.
@end deffn

@lisp
(defun worker (name)
  (loop (receive (m)
          ((eq m :crash) (error "~A crashed" name))
          ((eq m :stop) (return)))))

(start-supervisor
 (list (make-child-spec 'a 'worker :arguments '("a") :name 'a)
       (make-child-spec 'b 'worker :arguments '("b") :name 'b :restart :transient))
 :strategy :one-for-all)
(send 'a :crash)   ; a and b are both restarted
@end lisp

@subsection Conditions

@deffn Condition process-error
Base class of the module's errors; reader @code{process-error-process}.
Subclasses: @code{no-current-process-error} (an operation that needs
a calling process was called from a plain thread),
@code{dead-process-error}, @code{unknown-name-error} and
@code{name-taken-error} (readers @code{unknown-name-error-name},
@code{name-taken-error-name}), @code{call-timeout},
@code{call-failed} (reader @code{call-failed-reason}),
@code{server-start-error} (reader @code{server-start-error-reason})
and @code{supervisor-error}.
@end deffn

@deffn Condition process-exit-signal
Signaled in a process to make it exit; readers
@code{process-exit-signal-from} and @code{process-exit-signal-reason}.
It is a @code{serious-condition} but not an @code{error}, so that
handlers for errors in process code do not intercept it.
@end deffn

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item @strong{Scheduling is cooperative.}  A process is preempted only
at message operations, sleeps and explicit yields; an exit signal
reaches a running process at its next such point.  A process in a
loop with none of them cannot be stopped, and a supervisor that fails
to stop a child within its shutdown time gives up on it.
@item @strong{Process heaps.}  With a heap of its own, a process may
not store a pointer to its objects anywhere global: the store barrier
of @code{sb-fiber} signals @code{process-heap-store-error}.  Global
bookkeeping done on behalf of a process, such as a counter in a global
variable, must run inside @code{sb-fiber:without-heap}.  Closures
created in a process are process objects and cannot be spawned or sent;
use named functions and arguments.  Messages are copied, so a large
message costs a copy; @code{sb-fiber:make-shared-binary} makes a
payload that is sent by reference.
@item @strong{Blocking.}  Waiting on a mutex, a semaphore or I/O from a
process blocks its carrier and every process on it.  Use
@code{join-process} only from threads that are not processes.
@item @strong{Names.}  @code{process-p}, @code{process-alive-p} and
@code{process-error} also name functions in @code{sb-ext} (for
@code{run-program}); a package that uses both must shadow them, and
@code{cast} clashes with @code{sb-alien:cast}.
@item @strong{Images.}  Like fibers, processes do not survive
@code{save-lisp-and-die}; stop the scheduler before saving.
@end itemize
